xnu 0402: every process that draws a picture gets the picture bridge

A layer's `contents` is a CGImage for every image UIKit and an app draw, and on
this port a CGImage does not cross to the window server: the server creates a
surface of the picture's size and leaves it all zero (measured 2026-11-19 on a
640x480 picture and again 2026-11-20 on the 129x129 knob of Settings' Airplane
Mode switch, whose row draws its track and no knob). What draws is a client
surface, so tools/mondrian-layer-bridge.m converts every CGImage to one.

The first version matched `/Applications/`, which covers the system apps and
nothing else. An App Store app runs from
`/private/var/containers/Bundle/Application/<uuid>/Name.app/Name`, an extension
from `.../Name.app/PlugIns/X.appex/X`, and a page's pictures arrive as layer
contents in `com.apple.WebKit.Networking`, whose path is inside
WebKit.framework. None of those is under `/Applications/`, so a third-party app
and a web page still lost every picture. Measured 2026-11-22: the bridge ran in
Preferences, SpringBoard and Spotlight only.

The match is now two markers on the whole path, `.app/` and
`/WebKit.framework/`, which is the coverage the picture path needs: every app
bundle the system can launch, its extensions, and the WebKit services. Bit 10
(0x400) still selects the dylib, so the boot mask keeps its value in
`tools/boards.py` (`mondrian_ui_hook`); nothing else changes.

The dylib is not the hook: it carries `setContents:`, `-contents` and the
surface cache, and none of the hook's sixteen dyld interposers (IORegistry,
sandbox, TCC, the Objective-C throw path, libc++'s filesystem). Loading those
into every app is a change no app agreed to. A boot without the dylib in
`/private/var/vm` leaves apps clean and says so once.

The match is on the *whole* path, and that is what took a boot to find:
`ni_cnd.cn_nameptr` has been advanced past every directory by namei, so the
first version compared the last component ("Preferences") against
"/Applications/" and could never match. No app ever received the bridge and the
ring said nothing, because a `wanted == 0` exec is silent. `cn_pnbuf`, the
caller's NUL-terminated copy of the path (`cn_pnlen` bytes), is the one that
still holds all of it. The branch prints the path it compared, so a boot that
still misses names the string.

The app's copy of the dylib also answers `-contents` with the picture while the
layer keeps the surface, because UIKit reads a converted layer back and asserts
(`-[_UIImageCGImageContent _initWithCGImageSource:CGImage:scale:]`, "imageRef
passed is not a CGImageRef", measured 2026-11-21: SpringBoard aborted `sig 6`
right after `exec Preferences`). CoreAnimation builds its render tree from its
own stored contents, so the frame path still gets the surface.

/test: open Settings and read the Airplane Mode row -- the knob must draw next
to the track, in both states, and the ring must show
`MONDRIAN HOOK: /Applications/Preferences.app/Preferences gets the picture
bridge` and `MONDRIAN BRIDGE[<pid>]: 129x129 as surface ...` from the
Preferences pid, with no `MONDRIAN SURF: backboardd create ... 129x129 fmt 0x0`
after the app's first frame, and no assertion or `CRASH SpringBoard`. Then open
an app installed from the App Store, and Safari with a local page: the ring must
name that app's container path and `com.apple.WebKit.Networking` as
bridge-carrying.

